## The One-Time Trust Setup

### K8s Side — What Gets Collected

The trust playbook runs on k8s-master1 first and collects 4 things:

**Cluster API endpoint** (`https://10.0.51.100:16443`) — the VIP address of the K8s API. When a pod
sends a JWT to Vault, Vault needs to call K8s back to verify it. This is the address Vault calls.

**K8s CA cert** — K8s uses its own internal CA (generated by kubeadm, nothing to do with FreeIPA).
When Vault makes an HTTPS call to K8s API, it needs to trust K8s API's TLS cert. So it needs the K8s
CA cert. Different cert from FreeIPA.

**vault-auth token** (`--duration=30000h` ≈ 3.4 years) — this is Vault's ID card to present to K8s.
When Vault calls K8s API to verify a pod's token, K8s asks "who are you?" Vault presents this token.
It belongs to the `vault-auth` service account in kube-system which has `system:auth-delegator` —
permission to review other tokens. Long-lived by design, one-time setup. Can only be captured at
creation time — if lost, create a new one.

**Issuer** (`https://kubernetes.default.svc.cluster.local`) — embedded inside every K8s JWT in the
`iss` field. When Vault receives a pod JWT, it checks: does the issuer match what I expect? Same
concept as the audience check in AWS OIDC. `disable_iss_validation=false` means DO validate it.

### Vault Side — What Gets Configured

```
vault auth enable kubernetes          → opens the auth method (like creating OIDC provider in AWS)

vault write auth/kubernetes/config    → tells Vault:
  kubernetes_host = k8s API endpoint  → "call THIS address to verify tokens"
  kubernetes_ca_cert = k8s CA cert    → "trust THIS cert when calling that address"
  token_reviewer_jwt = vault-auth tok → "use THIS to authenticate yourself to K8s"
  issuer = issuer URL                 → "expect THIS in the iss field of pod JWTs"
```

The `vars_prompt` for vault admin token in the playbook is intentional — this is a human-in-the-loop
one-time operation. Someone has to authenticate to Vault to configure it. Private: yes means it doesn't
echo on screen and isn't logged.

