## The TLS Picture — Three Different Things

This is where it gets confusing. Three separate cert things, none of them the same:

**`/etc/ipa/ca.crt` on all domain nodes** — the IPA CA public cert. Same content everywhere. Not
identity — it's the "I can verify signatures from this CA" file.

**`/opt/vault/tls/tls.crt` on vault nodes** — the Vault SERVICE identity cert. Signed by IPA CA.
Unique per node. Proves "I am vault1.lab.local" (and vault.lab.local via SAN).

**`/etc/kubernetes/pki/ca.crt` on k8s nodes** — K8s internal CA. Generated by kubeadm at cluster
init. Signs K8s API server certs. Completely separate from IPA. Nothing to do with FreeIPA.

Two TLS connections happen during the runtime flow, each needing a different CA to verify:

```
Vault-injector (in K8s) → https://vault.lab.local:8200
  needs: IPA CA cert to verify Vault's cert
  how: vault-ca K8s Secret mounted via tls-secret annotation → /vault/tls/ca.crt

Vault → https://10.0.51.100:16443 (K8s API)
  needs: K8s CA cert to verify K8s API's cert
  how: kubernetes_ca_cert parameter in auth/kubernetes/config
```

