## The VIP Cert Problem — Real Incident

When vault-injector was configured to call `https://vault.lab.local:8200` (the HA VIP), TLS failed:

```
SSL: no alternative certificate subject name matches target hostname 'vault.lab.local'
```

The existing vault certs only had SANs for vault1/vault2/vault3.lab.local. The VIP name
`vault.lab.local` wasn't in any cert. Vault's cert was valid, but it didn't cover the VIP name.

Fix required adding `-D vault.lab.local` to the `ipa-getcert request` command. But this wasn't
straightforward — FreeIPA CA refused to sign it until two `managedby` relationships were configured:

1. **Host managedby** — vault1/2/3 need permission to request certs containing `vault.lab.local` as SAN:
```bash
ipa host-add-managedby vault.lab.local --hosts=vault1.lab.local  # separate call per host
ipa host-add-managedby vault.lab.local --hosts=vault2.lab.local
ipa host-add-managedby vault.lab.local --hosts=vault3.lab.local
```

2. **Service managedby** — same for the vault service principal (and `service-add-managedby` doesn't
exist — had to use `service-mod --addattr`):
```bash
ipa service-mod vault/vault.lab.local \
  --addattr=managedby=fqdn=vault1.lab.local,cn=computers,cn=accounts,dc=lab,dc=local
```

Both relationships required. Either one alone was not enough. Task order mattered: service principals
must exist before adding service managedby.

